============================================================
SECTION A: MULTI-STEP DRIFT vs PER-STEP IDENTITY BASELINE
============================================================
The debate's Sec 3.4 diagnostic 1 requires the identity control reported
alongside EVERY drift number, not just horizon 1. At k=4 the frame stacks
s_t and s_{t+4} share zero overlapping frames (D1), so a persistently high
predictor similarity there is the strongest evidence available that the
model is not just exploiting frame-stack overlap.

  step 1 | predictor=0.9869  identity=0.9743  advantage=+0.0126  (shares 3/4 frames with input)
  step 2 | predictor=0.9779  identity=0.9605  advantage=+0.0173  (shares 2/4 frames with input)
  step 3 | predictor=0.9731  identity=0.9428  advantage=+0.0303  (shares 1/4 frames with input)
  step 4 | predictor=0.9621  identity=0.9215  advantage=+0.0406  (shares 0/4 frames with input) <-- zero frame overlap: overlap confound eliminated
  step 5 | predictor=0.9606  identity=0.9146  advantage=+0.0460  (shares 0/4 frames with input) <-- zero frame overlap: overlap confound eliminated

============================================================
SECTION B: COUNTERFACTUAL ACTION SENSITIVITY (per-game action range)
============================================================
eval_JEPA_new.py tests all 17 offsets mod 18 regardless of game. For a
Breakout sequence (4 valid actions) that means 14 of 17 'wrong' actions
were never valid for Breakout at all -- borrowed embedding rows trained on
other games entirely (D2). This restricts wrong-action sampling to each
sequence's own game's valid action count.

  correct-action similarity      : 0.9869
  avg wrong-action similarity     : 0.9812  (avg 8.4 valid alternatives tested per sample, range 1-17)
  max disruption (worst action)   : 0.9737
  avg action delta                : +0.0057
  max action delta                : +0.0131
  -> PASS: predictor is action-sensitive within each game's own valid action range.

============================================================
SECTION C: ACTION-CONDITIONAL DISPERSION
============================================================
Debate Sec 2.2, diagnostic 2: compare how much predictions move BETWEEN
actions against how much the predictor moves the latent AT ALL. If the
action token were purely decorative, spread-across-actions would be small
relative to the total predicted displacement from z_t.

  mean ||p(z,a) - p(z,a')|| across actions : 1.1002
  mean ||p(z,a) - z||  (total predicted move): 3.8512
  dispersion / total-change ratio            : 0.2857
  -> Action identity accounts for 28.6% of the predictor's total
     displacement magnitude.

============================================================
SECTION D: ATTENTION MASS ON THE ACTION AND ENVIRONMENT TOKENS
============================================================
Debate Sec 2.2, diagnostic 3: 'near-zero attention mass [on the action
token] is dispositive'. v2's predictor sequence is [env, action, 36 patches],
so the uniform baseline is 1/38 = 0.0263 and the env token is measured
alongside the action token. Their relative mass says whether the predictor
leans on WHAT was pressed or merely on WHICH GAME it is playing -- the
latter would be the dynamics-side analogue of the D3 recognition shortcut.

  layer 0: action token = 0.0438   env token = 0.0941  (uniform would be 0.0263)
  layer 1: action token = 0.0255   env token = 0.0222  (uniform would be 0.0263)  <-- at/below uniform baseline
  layer 2: action token = 0.0190   env token = 0.0214  (uniform would be 0.0263)  <-- at/below uniform baseline

  average across layers: action 0.0294  env 0.0459  (ratio env/action = 1.56x)

  -> WARNING: average attention mass on the action token (0.0294) is close to or below the uniform baseline (0.0263). Spatial tokens are not preferentially attending to the action.

